    "text": "All hops verified. Here's the analysis, with repo-relative paths from `/workspace`.\n\n## Setup: what `zvec.init(optimize_threads=4)` establishes\n\n`init()` puts `optimize_threads` into the config dict (`python/zvec/zvec.py:162-163,173`), the binding validates it is > 0 and stores it as `data.optimize_thread_count` (`src/binding/python/model/common/python_config.cc:153-158`), then applies it via `GlobalConfig::Instance().initialize(data)` (`python_config.cc:193`; default would have been `query_thread_count`, `src/db/common/config.cc:37,42`). On first use, `GlobalResource::initialize()` \u2014 guarded by `std::call_once` \u2014 constructs the global pool `optimize_thread_pool_ = new ailego::ThreadPool(optimize_thread_count(), \u2026)` (`src/db/common/global_resource.cc:21-29`; accessor `src/db/common/global_resource.h:31-34`). `ThreadPool` clamps the requested size to `[1, hardware_concurrency]` and spawns exactly that many workers (`src/ailego/parallel/thread_pool.cc:85-87,93-112`), so with `hardware_concurrency \u2265 4` the global pool has **4 workers**.\n\n## 1. Pool selection per invocation\n\n`CollectionImpl::optimize` passes `options.concurrency_` into `build_compact_task` (`src/db/collection.cc:952,989,1126-1204`), which threads it into the compact/index-rebuild tasks. Both vector-index merge entry points make the same choice:\n\n- `SegmentImpl::merge_vector_indexer` (`src/db/index/segment/segment.cc:1549-1575`)\n- `SegmentHelper::MergeWithOptionalReuse` (`src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:799-811`)\n\n```\nconcurrency == 0 \u2192 merge_options.pool = GlobalResource::Instance().optimize_thread_pool();\n                   merge_options.write_concurrency = pool->count();\nconcurrency != 0 \u2192 pool stays nullptr; write_concurrency = concurrency;\n```\n\n`VectorColumnIndexer::merge` forwards `{write_concurrency, pool}` to `Index::merge` (`src/db/index/column/vector_column/vector_column_indexer.cc:103-134`), where the final selection happens (`src/core/interface/index.cc:1416-1452`):\n\n- **A (`concurrency=0`)**: `options.pool` is the global pool \u2192 `reducer->set_thread_pool(options.pool)` (1437); `effective_write_concurrency = min(4, pool->count()) = 4` (1438-1439). The core merge runs on the **global 4-worker optimization pool**.\n- **B (`concurrency=2`)**: `options.pool == nullptr` \u2192 a **call-local** `ailego::ThreadPool(2, false)` is created, set on the reducer, and `effective_write_concurrency = local->count() = 2` (1441-1445). The `unique_ptr` is scoped to the merge call (\"must declare here to ensure its lifespan can cover reducer->reduce()\", 1433-1434) and is destroyed when `Index::merge` returns.\n\n**B does not change the global pool.** The global pool is created once (`call_once`) with 4 workers; the B path never reads or writes it \u2014 it only builds a temporary 2-worker pool for that merge. `effective_write_concurrency` also caps the reducer's add-thread fan-out via `PARAM_MIXED_STREAMER_REDUCER_NUM_OF_ADD_THREADS` (`index.cc:1450-1452`).\n\n## 2. Handoff to the target builder\n\n`MixedStreamerReducer::reduce` requires a pool (`src/core/mixed_reducer/mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:210-214`) and takes the builder-backed path `reduce_with_builder` (223, def. 672) \u2192 `index_build` (739, def. 742-777). There, the selected pool is wrapped once:\n\n- `threads = make_shared<BorrowedSingleQueueIndexThreads>(*thread_pool_)` (743-744) \u2014 an adapter holding the pool **by reference**, whose `count()`/`submit()`/`make_group()` delegate to it (`src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_threads.h:174-232`).\n- Optional conversion training immediately before the builder calls gets the **same handle**: `IndexConverter::TrainAndTransform(target_builder_converter_, target_holder, threads)` (746-747), which moves it into `converter->train(two_pass_holder, std::move(threads))`; the follow-up `transform` does not receive it (`src/core/framework/index_converter.cc:26-35`).\n- Then `target_builder_->train(threads, target_holder)` (760) and `target_builder_->build(std::move(threads), target_holder)` (768).\n\nSo A's builder stages run against the borrowed global 4-worker pool; B's against the borrowed local 2-worker pool. The distinction the question asks for: what the reducer guarantees is only that an `IndexThreads::Pointer` over the selected pool is *passed*. Consumption is implementation-specific \u2014 e.g., DiskANN's `train`/`build` skip their own-pool fallbacks because `threads` is non-null (`src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_builder.cc:657-663,714-720`) and submit `threads->count()` tasks in `build_internal`/`prune_internal` (304-316, 341-357) and use `threads->count()` in `generate_quantized_data` (429, 447-449); a different builder could accept the handle and parallelize less or not at all.\n\n## 3. DiskANN PQ training: the chunk-dispatch pool is *not* reused\n\n`DiskAnnBuilder::train_quantized_data` (diskann_builder.cc:378-427) does **not** hand the borrowed pool to the quantizer. It computes a worker count and lets the quantizer build its own pool:\n\n- `// The quantizer creates its own pool, so bound it by the caller's pool.` (391)\n- `pq_thread_count = max(1, min(build_thread_count_, threads->count()))` (392-393), passed as `qp.set(\"thread_count\", pq_thread_count)` (394) into `PqInt8Quantizer::init`, which reads it at `src/turbo/quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer.cc:142` (member default `thread_count_{0}`, \"0 = hardware_concurrency\", `pq_int8_quantizer.h:215-217`).\n- `PqInt8Quantizer::train` (232) \u2192 `train_all_chunks` (298, 305) creates a **fresh** `SingleQueueIndexThreads(thread_count_, false)` (322-323) \u2014 which owns a real new `ailego::ThreadPool`, mapping 0 \u2192 `hardware_concurrency` (`index_threads.h:115-118, 76-166`) \u2014 and distributes PQ chunks across that pool's workers (326-333).\n- `build_thread_count_` itself comes from `PARAM_DISKANN_BUILDER_THREAD_COUNT`, defaulting to `max(1, hardware_concurrency)` when 0/unset (diskann_builder.cc:51-55).\n\nNet effect: the PQ-training outer pool's size = `max(1, min(builder setting, caller pool count))`. Under **A**: `min(build_thread_count_, 4)` \u2192 4 when `build_thread_count_ \u2265 4` (the default here, since hardware_concurrency \u2265 4). Under **B**: `min(build_thread_count_, 2)` = 2. So the caller's pool influences PQ training only as an upper bound on a *separate, newly created* pool, while the borrowed pool remains the one driving graph build/prune/PQ-code generation (diskann_builder.cc:762,768,777).\n\n## 4. What the settings do \u2014 and don't \u2014 establish\n\n**Established (capacity bounds):**\n- Global optimize pool capacity = 4 workers (init + `hardware_concurrency \u2265 4`, no clamping); A's merge, and every `concurrency=0` merge, is bounded by it.\n- B's merge is bounded by a transient 2-worker local pool; the global pool is untouched and still has 4.\n- Every `ailego::ThreadPool` is clamped to `[1, hardware_concurrency]` (`thread_pool.cc:93-101`), and the PQ training pool is additionally bounded by `min(build_thread_count_, caller count)` (diskann_builder.cc:392-393).\n\n**Not established:**\n- **Whole-process thread count.** Configuring 4 optimize workers says nothing about the aggregate: the query pool is separate (`global_resource.cc:24-26`, default = cgroup CPU limit, `config.cc:37,42`); each PQ training call spins up an extra pool (`pq_int8_quantizer.cc:322-323`); builders invoked with null threads create their own (`diskann_builder.cc:657-659,714-716`); each `concurrency>0` merge temporarily adds a local pool; plus RocksDB, Python, and I/O threads.\n- **Actual CPU utilization.** Pool size is capacity, not demand: stages run sequentially (train \u2192 build \u2192 prune \u2192 PQ generate), workers idle between task groups, `build_internal`'s submitter blocks in a wait loop (`diskann_builder.cc:317-331`), and task counts may be below worker counts. Binding is off by default (`config.cc:39,43`), so nothing pins work to cores either. And per \u00a72, passing an `IndexThreads` handle proves availability of the pool to a builder, not that any particular implementation saturates it."
